Competing claims: (101,101) and (101,301) under-count the 3-cohesive demand of the fixed ballots; (601,601) and (200,300) mis-apply the quota (600/n instead of 6≥ℓ·n/100); (202,302) and (205,305) add spurious extra voters. No response refutes the 201/301 thresholds.  

Persistent open point: whether added ballots could introduce voters who approve both y and z, potentially lowering s2; all detailed arguments assume this is impossible without enlarging N* and therefore tightening the quota.}

