10.text:
(201, 301)

The fixed 6-voter group \(N^*=\{1,\dots,6\}\) has intersection size exactly 3 on \(\{a,b,c\}\) and union \(\{a,b,c,x,y,z\}\). Any size-100 committee leaving voter 1 unsatisfied intersects that union in at most \(\{y,z\}\). Under the standard (non-strict) quota \(|N^*|\ge\ell\cdot n/100\),

- \(N^*\) is 3-cohesive precisely for \(n\le200\), so both axioms are violated;
- at \(n=201\) the group drops to 2-cohesive; PJR is met by placing both \(y\) and \(z\) in \(W\) (the remaining 98 seats filled by fresh candidates each approved by suitably sized blocs among the 195 added voters);
- EJR additionally requires some single voter in \(N^*\) to receive two representatives personally, impossible until the group ceases to be 2-cohesive (i.e., only at \(n=301\)), at which point a single seat (\(y\) or \(z\)) suffices and the same padding meets every other EJR obligation.

No smaller extension works, because added ballots can only raise \(n\) and therefore can only weaken (never strengthen) the demands of the fixed ballots. All competing thresholds contain quota-boundary, per-voter vs. collective, or padding-count errors.

